1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,800
This patient is about to receive a dose

2
00:00:08,801 --> 00:00:11,600
of high-technology treatment.

3
00:00:11,601 --> 00:00:12,600
Start the scan.

4
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:20,400
Computer tomography scanning, known as CT,

5
00:00:20,401 --> 00:00:24,600
or CAT scanning, explores inside a human body

6
00:00:24,601 --> 00:00:28,400
and is a life-saving tool for diagnosing disease.

7
00:00:28,401 --> 00:00:32,880
But in this case, the patient is dead.

8
00:00:32,881 --> 00:00:35,080
In fact, dead for 5,000 years.

9
00:00:35,081 --> 00:00:39,320
But hopefully, the CT scan will tell us why he died

10
00:00:39,321 --> 00:00:43,080
and what conditions may have led to his death.

11
00:00:43,081 --> 00:00:45,760
This mummy from the Michael C. Carlos Museum

12
00:00:45,761 --> 00:00:50,080
in Atlanta, Georgia, is examined without being unwrapped

13
00:00:50,081 --> 00:00:51,760
to uncover the secrets inside.

14
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:56,760
Looks like there is some tissue there.

15
00:00:56,761 --> 00:00:57,760
Wow.

16
00:00:57,761 --> 00:01:00,200
Oh, that's really interesting.

17
00:01:00,201 --> 00:01:04,060
CT scanning is based on the x-ray principle.

18
00:01:04,061 --> 00:01:06,740
As x-rays pass through the body, they're

19
00:01:06,741 --> 00:01:13,300
absorbed at differing levels, creating a flat image on film.

20
00:01:13,301 --> 00:01:16,460
In the case of the CT, the x-rays

21
00:01:16,461 --> 00:01:20,200
are fired from various angles around the subject

22
00:01:20,201 --> 00:01:24,000
to create a cross-section, or slice,

23
00:01:24,001 --> 00:01:26,340
as the patient moves through the scanner

24
00:01:26,341 --> 00:01:29,740
up to 1,500 slices are produced and stacked together,

25
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:31,400
like a deck of cards,

26
00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:34,100
to form a comprehensive picture of the body.

27
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:38,720
Technology has really revolutionized

28
00:01:38,721 --> 00:01:42,100
both the study of mummies and dealing with them in museums

29
00:01:42,101 --> 00:01:45,580
because it helps us not only understand how they were made,

30
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:48,320
but it helps us preserve them for future generations.

31
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:51,740
Across the country,

32
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:55,480
at the Rosicrucian Egyptian Museum in San Jose, California,

33
00:01:55,481 --> 00:02:02,480
A curiously tiny mummy has been on display for more than 75 years.

34
00:02:02,481 --> 00:02:13,480
The mystery of this small mummy is a story of who this was, how this person lived, and

35
00:02:13,481 --> 00:02:14,701
how this person died.

36
00:02:14,702 --> 00:02:18,480
And given that this is a small mummy, the assumption was a child.

37
00:02:18,481 --> 00:02:25,480
To unlock the mystery beneath the bandages, Dr. Amy Ladd, along with a team

38
00:02:25,481 --> 00:02:29,540
of scanning and computer experts, performed one of the most

39
00:02:29,541 --> 00:02:33,560
sophisticated high-tech medical exams ever carried out

40
00:02:33,561 --> 00:02:35,700
on an Egyptian mummy.

41
00:02:35,701 --> 00:02:37,440
This technology is fascinating.

42
00:02:37,441 --> 00:02:40,500
It's like doing a virtual autopsy without touching

43
00:02:40,501 --> 00:02:43,420
the mummy at all.

44
00:02:43,421 --> 00:02:48,380
The type of scanner used on the mummy is called a C-arm.

45
00:02:48,381 --> 00:02:50,160
It's a movable arm that completely

46
00:02:50,161 --> 00:02:53,580
encircles the subject.

47
00:02:53,581 --> 00:02:57,340
the C-Arm produces an astounding 60,000 image slices,

48
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:02,340
which deliver details rarely before seen in a mummy scan.

49
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:05,820
This is breakthrough technology.

50
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:09,400
Humans cannot even go through the scanner right now.

51
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:10,340
It's that new.

52
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:13,240
The thousands of images from the scanner

53
00:03:13,241 --> 00:03:15,020
were then fed into a supercomputer

54
00:03:15,021 --> 00:03:20,160
to create a virtual three-dimensional image inside the mummy.

55
00:03:20,161 --> 00:03:26,840
It's a fantastic thing because it's preserved in its original state.

56
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:31,660
And as we found out, it is preserved so well that we can see details as if it were a child

57
00:03:31,661 --> 00:03:33,700
that we're seeing in the clinic today.

58
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:36,832
With these astounding images, Dr.

59
00:03:36,833 --> 00:03:40,480
Ladd was able to solve the mystery of the tiny mummy.

60
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:45,360
First, by examining the 3,000-year-old teeth.

61
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:49,220
This is the mummy scan of the jaw.

62
00:03:49,221 --> 00:03:50,520
This is from the side.

63
00:03:51,220 --> 00:03:55,920
You see the lower jaw, baby teeth, which are already in place,

64
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:59,960
and the teeth that haven't come in yet that are beginning to form,

65
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:01,900
including the six-year molars.

66
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:05,660
This tells us the age because there's baby teeth still in place,

67
00:04:05,740 --> 00:04:09,000
so we know it's a young child, hasn't lost any teeth.

68
00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:11,220
The six-year molars haven't come in yet,

69
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:13,380
which tells that this child is younger than six.

70
00:04:13,381 --> 00:04:20,380
Details from under the wrappings also revealed clues about the sex of the child.

71
00:04:20,381 --> 00:04:25,380
So this image of the mummy skull, the child's skull.

72
00:04:25,381 --> 00:04:29,380
We know it's a girl because on the skull there is a wavy pattern,

73
00:04:29,381 --> 00:04:32,380
which is the preservation of the child's hair.

74
00:04:32,381 --> 00:04:36,380
And at that time, a girl had hair and a boy had his head shaved.

75
00:04:36,381 --> 00:04:41,380
We also know it's a girl because there is no mummified genitalia between the legs,

76
00:04:41,381 --> 00:04:44,640
between the legs, which by default, that's a girl and not a boy.

77
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:50,800
The scanning images were even able to tell the story of how the little girl may have died.

78
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:58,240
There's a suggestion of there's some scalloping out these kind of grayish areas,

79
00:04:58,241 --> 00:05:03,440
which possibly means there was increased intracranial pressure, meaning this child

80
00:05:03,441 --> 00:05:07,520
may have had a virus or a bacteria infection causing swelling in the brain,

81
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:08,920
which may have been the cause of death.

82
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:13,220
So it was really a tragedy.

83
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:15,980
There was this healthy child probably running around

84
00:05:15,981 --> 00:05:17,740
until just a few weeks before her death.

85
00:05:18,120 --> 00:05:19,660
And what an abrupt ending.

86
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:24,220
This is a kid who had a whole life to live, which wasn't.

87
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:26,020
And it's preserved in time.

88
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:28,400
And that's been a wonderful experience

89
00:05:28,401 --> 00:05:29,400
unraveling that mystery.

90
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:35,080
Our high tech may help us understand the past.

91
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:38,720
But how did the Egyptians use their technology

92
00:05:38,721 --> 00:05:43,680
to accomplish some of the most amazing body preservation the world has ever known.

93
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:46,920
The Egyptians were the techies of their day.

94
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:49,640
They had skills that nobody else had.

95
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:53,340
Dr. Robert Breyer, a renowned Egyptologist,

96
00:05:54,380 --> 00:05:59,800
is considered by many to be the world's foremost expert on mummies and the mummification process.

97
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:01,480
In fact...

98
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:05,240
People sometimes call me Mr. Mummy. I'd never call myself that.

99
00:06:05,241 --> 00:06:07,680
But I think it's that they're surprised that there's anybody

100
00:06:07,681 --> 00:06:08,940
who would work with just mummies.

101
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:12,220
Dr. Breyer is so fascinated by mummies,

102
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:16,280
he wanted to accomplish what no one else had in thousands of years.

103
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:24,000
In 1994, he performed and documented an actual body mummification,

104
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:27,340
using the same process as the ancient Egyptians.

105
00:06:28,900 --> 00:06:31,400
I decided to mummify a human cadaver

106
00:06:31,401 --> 00:06:35,160
because I wanted to know exactly how the Egyptians mummified their dead.

107
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:38,440
Without the use of modern medical technology,

108
00:06:39,580 --> 00:06:41,780
Dr. Breyer set out to mummify a body

109
00:06:41,781 --> 00:06:45,680
using the exact tools available to the Egyptians millennia ago.

110
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:50,400
In order to do the project, we had to replicate Egyptian tools.

111
00:06:51,060 --> 00:06:54,700
Now, they had knives made out of bronze and copper,

112
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:58,360
but they also had obsidian, which is volcanic glass,

113
00:06:58,580 --> 00:07:01,860
and that's very sharp, even sharper than surgical scalpels.

114
00:07:01,861 --> 00:07:03,740
So they used that for incisions, too.

115
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:06,740
Using a cadaver donated to science,

116
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:11,320
Dr. Breyer began his first step in the Egyptian mummification process,

117
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:16,400
removing the internal organs, which are prone to rapid decay.

118
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:23,060
In order to preserve a body, you have to make sure that bacteria don't act on it.

119
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:24,600
Bacteria is what causes decay.

120
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:26,280
They act on the soft tissue.

121
00:07:26,720 --> 00:07:29,540
And the reason they go to the soft tissue is there's water there.

122
00:07:29,541 --> 00:07:34,980
So the key is dehydrate quickly, get rid of the moisture so the bacteria won't decay your body.

123
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:37,860
Now this tool was really important.

124
00:07:38,580 --> 00:07:42,900
They removed the internal organs through a tiny incision, only three and a half inches wide.

125
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:45,460
So you can't get your full hands, you can't get two hands in.

126
00:07:45,760 --> 00:07:51,540
So this tool, when you've got your incision made, you can put it in the incision and kind of pull the organs out.

127
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:58,940
The Egyptians made such small incisions because they wanted to cause bodies as little damage as possible.

128
00:07:59,540 --> 00:08:05,380
Ancient embalmers removed all the internal organs,

129
00:08:05,381 --> 00:08:11,260
from the stomach and lungs to the liver, pancreas, and gallbladder.

130
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:14,320
The only organ left was the heart.

131
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:19,280
They left the heart in place because they believed you thought with your heart.

132
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:21,280
They didn't realize you thought with your brain.

133
00:08:21,740 --> 00:08:25,760
So they left the heart in place because you're going to need that to do your thinking when you get to the next world.

134
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:28,500
Once the organs were removed,

135
00:08:29,160 --> 00:08:32,400
the Egyptians stored them and buried them with the mummy

136
00:08:32,401 --> 00:08:35,300
in containers called canopic jars.

137
00:08:37,080 --> 00:08:38,240
It's really a jar.

138
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:39,180
It's got a lid.

139
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:40,400
It's hollow inside.

140
00:08:41,300 --> 00:08:43,240
And the lid is in the shape of a god

141
00:08:43,241 --> 00:08:44,500
who protects the internal organs.

142
00:08:45,180 --> 00:08:48,420
And the ancient Egyptians put the internal organs inside

143
00:08:48,421 --> 00:08:49,340
for protection.

144
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:53,240
The early morticians didn't preserve the brain

145
00:08:53,241 --> 00:08:56,820
because they thought it was responsible for producing mucus.

146
00:08:57,600 --> 00:08:59,900
So to keep skull damage to a minimum,

147
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:03,620
they removed the brain through the nose.

148
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:06,920
Undoubtedly, the hardest thing for the Egyptians to do

149
00:09:06,921 --> 00:09:08,180
was remove the brain.

150
00:09:09,060 --> 00:09:10,860
Now, this was done by a skilled surgeon,

151
00:09:11,020 --> 00:09:12,340
somebody who really knew what he was doing.

152
00:09:14,120 --> 00:09:16,600
The surgeon opened a pathway to the brain

153
00:09:16,601 --> 00:09:18,720
by breaking through the ethmoid bone

154
00:09:18,721 --> 00:09:20,200
in the back of the nasal cavity.

155
00:09:20,201 --> 00:09:24,600
And then they used something like this.

156
00:09:24,601 --> 00:09:26,200
I mean, it looks like a coat hanger.

157
00:09:26,201 --> 00:09:28,200
And what they did was they inserted it

158
00:09:28,201 --> 00:09:30,200
through the nasal passages into the cranium.

159
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:36,200
The brain doesn't stick to the tool.

160
00:09:36,201 --> 00:09:37,200
It's almost too liquid.

161
00:09:37,201 --> 00:09:41,200
So what we realized is that the way the Egyptians did it was

162
00:09:41,201 --> 00:09:44,200
they indeed put the long coat hanger light instrument

163
00:09:44,201 --> 00:09:45,200
through the nose.

164
00:09:45,201 --> 00:09:48,200
But then once it's in the cranium, in the brain,

165
00:09:48,201 --> 00:09:51,240
brain, you use it like a kitchen whisk almost.

166
00:09:51,241 --> 00:09:54,060
We rotated it around and around and around,

167
00:09:54,061 --> 00:09:57,020
breaking down the brain, liquefying it.

168
00:09:57,021 --> 00:09:59,600
Then you turn the cadaver upside down,

169
00:09:59,601 --> 00:10:02,240
and the brain runs out through the nose like a liquid.

170
00:10:02,241 --> 00:10:04,620
That's how the Egyptians did it.

171
00:10:04,621 --> 00:10:06,780
The ancient embalmers removed moisture

172
00:10:06,781 --> 00:10:09,140
from inside the body by inserting

173
00:10:09,141 --> 00:10:15,060
pouches of a sodium carbonate compound called natrin.

174
00:10:15,061 --> 00:10:16,820
This is natrin.

175
00:10:16,821 --> 00:10:20,700
It's exactly like the natrin the ancient Egyptian embalmers used 2,000 years ago.

176
00:10:21,520 --> 00:10:24,900
Natrin is basically a salt, and the way it dehydrates is like any salt.

177
00:10:24,901 --> 00:10:25,960
It's hygroscopic.

178
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:31,260
It pulls the water out of the body, bonds with it, and the body becomes dehydrated.

179
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:34,200
Then, Dr.

180
00:10:34,201 --> 00:10:39,560
Breyer copied the Egyptian practice of covering the outside of the body with natrin

181
00:10:39,561 --> 00:10:43,200
and leaving it to dry in the heat for 35 days.

182
00:10:43,201 --> 00:10:53,860
the result a dried out but recognizable body this in essence is what a mummy would have looked like

183
00:10:53,861 --> 00:10:58,620
thousands of years ago before the final and most recognizable step in mummification

184
00:10:58,621 --> 00:11:01,980
wrapping the deceased in linen lots of linen

185
00:11:01,981 --> 00:11:09,080
and wrapping was a ritual they wrapped each finger individually

186
00:11:09,081 --> 00:11:14,900
the hands, the legs.

187
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:18,460
There are magical spells on the bandages.

188
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:21,900
As this is going on, priests are chanting prayers,

189
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:26,120
so there is a ritual that's going on parallel to the physical process.

190
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:32,120
Over 20 pounds of linen wrappings were used during a typical Egyptian mummification.

191
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:35,540
If you were wealthy enough, as you were being wrapped,

192
00:11:35,541 --> 00:11:37,880
within the layers of wrappings,

193
00:11:38,380 --> 00:11:39,980
they would place magical amulets.

194
00:11:40,360 --> 00:11:42,080
Now, an amulet is anything that's small

195
00:11:42,081 --> 00:11:43,600
that can be worn for protection.

196
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:46,260
So in the bandages,

197
00:11:46,540 --> 00:11:48,460
you could have as many as 100 amulets.

198
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:51,600
This is an ancient amulet from a mummy.

199
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:53,140
It's 3,000 years old.

200
00:11:53,560 --> 00:11:55,020
And if you look at it closely,

201
00:11:55,580 --> 00:11:57,420
you can see that it's carved like a beetle.

202
00:11:58,220 --> 00:12:00,100
And on the bottom is the payoff.

203
00:12:00,101 --> 00:12:01,600
There are two little holes.

204
00:12:02,340 --> 00:12:03,880
That's where it was sewn onto the mummy

205
00:12:03,881 --> 00:12:05,240
to protect it forever.

206
00:12:05,540 --> 00:12:06,540
It was in the wrappings.

207
00:12:07,200 --> 00:12:10,640
The wrappings were coated with resin made from tree sap.

208
00:12:11,800 --> 00:12:15,140
The resin hardened into a black tar-like substance,

209
00:12:15,580 --> 00:12:18,200
which ensured the wrappings stayed in place.

210
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:23,820
The word mummy comes from the Arabic word mummiyah,

211
00:12:24,320 --> 00:12:25,540
which means black tar.

212
00:12:27,140 --> 00:12:31,500
The entire process took the Egyptians up to 70 days to complete.

213
00:12:32,900 --> 00:12:34,480
For these modern-day mummifiers,

214
00:12:34,481 --> 00:12:39,040
It was an education in a long-lost ancient art.

215
00:12:40,460 --> 00:12:42,800
I think the mummy project was really important.

216
00:12:43,480 --> 00:12:45,320
It was the first time in 2,000 years

217
00:12:45,321 --> 00:12:48,040
that anyone had conducted an Egyptian mummification.

218
00:12:48,720 --> 00:12:52,560
And for the first time, we know the details now of how they did it.

219
00:12:52,760 --> 00:12:54,080
So I think it was really important.

220
00:12:56,580 --> 00:12:59,500
Although the process was over for Dr. Breyer,

221
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:05,040
The ancients still had much to do to protect the mummy for eternity.

222
00:13:07,240 --> 00:13:12,440
In addition to the wrappings, the Egyptians used a papier-mâché-like material

223
00:13:12,441 --> 00:13:16,860
called a cartonnage to cover the head and feet of the mummy.

224
00:13:18,540 --> 00:13:21,000
Then the body was placed in a wooden coffin.

225
00:13:22,220 --> 00:13:25,800
And finally, if the deceased had been wealthy or a pharaoh,

226
00:13:26,560 --> 00:13:29,860
The mummy was sealed in a stone or granite sarcophagus.

227
00:13:31,620 --> 00:13:33,560
So it's kind of like a whole set of luggage

228
00:13:33,561 --> 00:13:34,900
that fits one inside the other.

229
00:13:35,140 --> 00:13:37,400
You've got all of these different layers of protection,

230
00:13:37,760 --> 00:13:38,500
all for eternity.

231
00:13:41,780 --> 00:13:44,100
Although mummification was a complicated,

232
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:45,500
time-consuming task,

233
00:13:46,100 --> 00:13:48,280
it was only the start of a burial ritual.

234
00:13:50,660 --> 00:13:52,600
The Egyptians honored the dead

235
00:13:52,601 --> 00:13:55,000
by constructing enormous tombs and temples.

236
00:13:55,800 --> 00:13:57,760
which became wonders of the world.

237
00:14:05,540 --> 00:14:07,960
Egypt is the only country in the world

238
00:14:07,961 --> 00:14:11,520
where the process of preserving a body became an art form.

239
00:14:14,740 --> 00:14:19,620
The Egyptians began to develop their skills before 3,000 B.C.

240
00:14:21,720 --> 00:14:23,440
Mummification actually was discovered

241
00:14:23,441 --> 00:14:25,560
sort of by accident by the ancient Egyptians.

242
00:14:25,800 --> 00:14:27,520
This is such a hot, dry climate.

243
00:14:28,080 --> 00:14:29,460
The sand is virtually an oven.

244
00:14:30,580 --> 00:14:33,380
They buried people in graves in the ground

245
00:14:33,381 --> 00:14:36,760
and then discovered that these bodies were pretty much preserved,

246
00:14:37,060 --> 00:14:39,600
almost like dried fish.

247
00:14:40,140 --> 00:14:43,080
And they realized that nature could do this

248
00:14:43,081 --> 00:14:45,340
and then began to develop technologies

249
00:14:45,341 --> 00:14:47,520
and a pretty sophisticated science

250
00:14:47,521 --> 00:14:50,120
to be able to sort of artificially do the same thing.

251
00:14:51,100 --> 00:14:53,320
As it evolved over the centuries,

252
00:14:54,080 --> 00:14:56,520
mummification grew into a vast industry.

253
00:14:57,600 --> 00:14:59,060
When it came to mummification,

254
00:14:59,640 --> 00:15:02,400
the Egyptians were way ahead of everybody in the Near East.

255
00:15:02,960 --> 00:15:04,260
Nobody could do it like they did.

256
00:15:04,340 --> 00:15:05,800
They also had the resources.

257
00:15:06,320 --> 00:15:08,840
They were a wealthy country that had conquered the Near East,

258
00:15:09,020 --> 00:15:11,040
so it takes a lot of money to do this,

259
00:15:11,240 --> 00:15:12,500
and they had the money to do it,

260
00:15:12,501 --> 00:15:16,000
so they could indulge themselves and really perfect the art form.

261
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:21,060
The Egyptians utilized their technology and resources

262
00:15:21,061 --> 00:15:22,660
not only for mummification,

263
00:15:22,661 --> 00:15:26,340
but also to build enormous tombs.

264
00:15:27,140 --> 00:15:30,700
A vast complex of these tombs is known as a necropolis

265
00:15:30,701 --> 00:15:32,800
or city of the dead.

266
00:15:35,240 --> 00:15:38,260
The design and technology to make the tombs

267
00:15:38,261 --> 00:15:40,760
was pretty sophisticated and pretty demanding.

268
00:15:41,020 --> 00:15:42,900
They were working with bronze tools

269
00:15:42,901 --> 00:15:45,460
hewing out square chambers in the rock,

270
00:15:45,840 --> 00:15:49,480
sometimes hundreds of yards deep into the limestone.

271
00:15:49,481 --> 00:15:54,320
And typically, a pharaoh had this team working for 40 years to prepare his tomb.

272
00:15:54,760 --> 00:15:57,740
That's a remarkable feat of skill and engineering,

273
00:15:58,140 --> 00:15:59,580
considering what they had at their disposal.

274
00:16:04,460 --> 00:16:08,440
The Great Pyramid of Khufu, one of the seven wonders of the world,

275
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:13,200
is a tomb constructed utilizing this organization and building technology.

276
00:16:13,201 --> 00:16:21,740
It is still the single largest masonry building ever constructed.

277
00:16:22,140 --> 00:16:24,760
And all of that was done to house in the center

278
00:16:24,761 --> 00:16:28,420
a little tiny burial chamber with the pharaoh's mummy inside.

279
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:35,700
The Egyptians believed that a dead person's soul left the body,

280
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:39,220
but would rejoin it at burial.

281
00:16:39,221 --> 00:16:45,120
So the deceased needed his body and worldly possessions for the afterlife.

282
00:16:47,300 --> 00:16:52,620
The basic design for most Egyptian tombs included three essential compartments,

283
00:16:53,400 --> 00:16:58,180
the entrance, the offering chamber, and the burial chamber.

284
00:16:59,300 --> 00:17:02,740
This is a typical entrance to an ancient Egyptian tomb.

285
00:17:03,160 --> 00:17:07,200
These hieroglyphs would tell you who was buried in this tomb,

286
00:17:07,201 --> 00:17:10,980
would also present a welcome and a curse.

287
00:17:11,760 --> 00:17:14,700
The curse or the warning in the hieroglyph says,

288
00:17:15,100 --> 00:17:16,800
if you come with bad intentions,

289
00:17:17,280 --> 00:17:19,480
depart now before it is too late.

290
00:17:19,820 --> 00:17:22,360
But we have good intentions, so let's go inside.

291
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:32,660
Tomb builders carved a tunnel

292
00:17:32,661 --> 00:17:35,540
that connected the entrance to the first room inside.

293
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:40,400
the offering chamber.

294
00:17:40,401 --> 00:17:44,240
A door like this would have been found in an ancient tomb.

295
00:17:44,241 --> 00:17:46,560
The relatives of the deceased person

296
00:17:46,561 --> 00:17:50,260
would leave offerings here at the bottom of the door.

297
00:17:50,261 --> 00:17:55,960
And the deceased person's soul would magically enter here

298
00:17:55,961 --> 00:17:58,860
and take the food or other offerings

299
00:17:58,861 --> 00:18:02,540
to the afterlife back out through this same magical door.

300
00:18:07,200 --> 00:18:11,500
The Egyptians dug a shaft, sometimes hundreds of feet long,

301
00:18:11,600 --> 00:18:13,740
that connected to the final chamber.

302
00:18:16,380 --> 00:18:18,440
We're in the burial chamber now.

303
00:18:18,540 --> 00:18:21,340
This is the granite sarcophagus.

304
00:18:21,440 --> 00:18:24,580
Inside of this would have been a wooden sarcophagus,

305
00:18:24,680 --> 00:18:26,920
and inside of that would have been placed the body

306
00:18:27,020 --> 00:18:28,160
of the tomb owner.

307
00:18:28,260 --> 00:18:32,160
All of this then would have been buried underground.

308
00:18:32,260 --> 00:18:35,920
The hieroglyphs and images in the burial chamber

309
00:18:35,921 --> 00:18:40,120
the burial chamber described the life that the tomb owner wanted

310
00:18:40,121 --> 00:18:43,120
to live in the afterlife.

311
00:18:43,121 --> 00:18:46,360
Included in the burial chamber would have been items that the

312
00:18:46,361 --> 00:18:50,560
deceased person would hope to use in the next life, treasures

313
00:18:50,561 --> 00:18:55,040
that they would take with them to the afterlife.

314
00:18:55,041 --> 00:18:57,600
The enormous treasures became easy targets

315
00:18:57,601 --> 00:18:59,640
for grave robbers.

316
00:18:59,641 --> 00:19:02,920
So tomb builders created a barricade to protect these

317
00:19:02,921 --> 00:19:06,920
by backfilling the passages to the burial chamber.

318
00:19:06,921 --> 00:19:08,920
So the first thing a thief encountered when he found

319
00:19:08,921 --> 00:19:14,920
the tomb site was sometimes 300 yards of solid stone rubble

320
00:19:14,921 --> 00:19:18,920
that he had to dig through to get into the tomb proper.

321
00:19:18,921 --> 00:19:21,920
Over the centuries, the interiors of tombs became

322
00:19:21,921 --> 00:19:25,920
increasingly elaborate, with the intention of confusing tomb

323
00:19:25,921 --> 00:19:27,920
writers.

324
00:19:27,921 --> 00:19:31,920
Often Egyptian tombs were designed specifically to,

325
00:19:31,921 --> 00:19:35,600
to hide the mummy, to keep the mummy secret.

326
00:19:35,940 --> 00:19:39,880
So one of the manners was to use false passages,

327
00:19:40,540 --> 00:19:43,460
false doors, false chambers, et cetera,

328
00:19:43,760 --> 00:19:46,460
so that people would be lost, in effect,

329
00:19:46,640 --> 00:19:47,980
kind of in a maze, often.

330
00:19:49,140 --> 00:19:51,800
However, this did little to deter grave robbing.

331
00:19:54,120 --> 00:19:57,200
Thieves often desecrated the mummies and their tombs

332
00:19:57,201 --> 00:20:00,720
to steal the riches that were meant for the afterlife.

333
00:20:01,920 --> 00:20:05,380
The effect of the plundering of the tomb over the centuries

334
00:20:05,381 --> 00:20:09,020
was that an untold number of ancient riches

335
00:20:09,021 --> 00:20:11,620
have been lost or dispersed.

336
00:20:12,420 --> 00:20:15,860
A threat greater than grave robbing was yet to come.

337
00:20:17,760 --> 00:20:22,220
In 525 BC, the Persians conquered Egypt,

338
00:20:23,260 --> 00:20:26,120
followed by the Greeks and the Romans.

339
00:20:27,860 --> 00:20:30,840
The invaders were fascinated by mummification

340
00:20:30,841 --> 00:20:33,540
and allowed the practice to continue.

341
00:20:35,540 --> 00:20:40,340
But later in 642 AD, the Arabs occupied Egypt

342
00:20:40,341 --> 00:20:42,840
and Islam became the dominant religion.

343
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:48,420
Mummification was not tolerated and came to an end.

344
00:20:49,760 --> 00:20:51,500
And then it became a lost art.

345
00:20:52,320 --> 00:20:54,540
Egypt was eventually invaded by the Arabs,

346
00:20:55,280 --> 00:20:56,560
becomes a Muslim country,

347
00:20:57,040 --> 00:20:59,940
and in a sense is cut off from the Christian West.

348
00:20:59,941 --> 00:21:02,620
So throughout the Middle Ages, throughout the Renaissance,

349
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:04,360
we've lost touch with Egypt,

350
00:21:04,620 --> 00:21:06,520
and that art of mummification is gone.

351
00:21:07,440 --> 00:21:11,660
So sands come in, whole temples are lost,

352
00:21:12,240 --> 00:21:13,560
tombs are buried under sand,

353
00:21:13,920 --> 00:21:15,360
and it simply remains there,

354
00:21:15,720 --> 00:21:17,400
waiting for archaeologists to discover it.

355
00:21:20,160 --> 00:21:23,140
With the advance of another conquering superpower,

356
00:21:24,180 --> 00:21:26,240
Egypt's mummies would be rediscovered.

357
00:21:26,241 --> 00:21:30,880
In 1798, Napoleon invaded Egypt

358
00:21:30,881 --> 00:21:34,580
and took scientists with him to document Egyptian culture.

359
00:21:35,500 --> 00:21:39,160
And they really do the first archaeological survey of Egypt.

360
00:21:39,580 --> 00:21:42,880
And this is how the West really learns about Egypt for the first time.

361
00:21:43,360 --> 00:21:46,460
When they start publishing their drawings and their paintings

362
00:21:46,461 --> 00:21:50,320
and people see for the first time accurate depictions of mummies,

363
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,800
this is when Europe goes wild with Egyptomania.

364
00:21:53,801 --> 00:21:57,160
In the 19th and early 20th centuries,

365
00:21:58,080 --> 00:22:00,580
Egyptian mummies became historical treasures

366
00:22:00,581 --> 00:22:03,500
sought after by museums worldwide.

367
00:22:04,100 --> 00:22:06,540
Once people realize how important mummies are,

368
00:22:06,940 --> 00:22:07,900
that they're part of history,

369
00:22:08,420 --> 00:22:10,340
then they start being displayed in museums,

370
00:22:11,100 --> 00:22:12,440
being taken care of,

371
00:22:13,020 --> 00:22:15,280
and then that leads to studying them.

372
00:22:15,960 --> 00:22:17,560
During the 1900s,

373
00:22:17,920 --> 00:22:20,520
archaeologists combed Egyptian burial sites

374
00:22:20,521 --> 00:22:22,060
for these links to the past.

375
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:25,560
One of the most important discoveries

376
00:22:25,561 --> 00:22:29,000
was the tomb of King Tut.

377
00:22:29,001 --> 00:22:31,300
But there was more than treasure here.

378
00:22:31,301 --> 00:22:34,040
There was a curse and a murder mystery.

379
00:22:39,040 --> 00:22:44,280
King Tutankhamun, or as he is best known, King Tut,

380
00:22:44,281 --> 00:22:46,680
is probably the most famous mummy in the world.

381
00:22:50,560 --> 00:22:52,320
The pharaoh is not only celebrated

382
00:22:52,321 --> 00:22:56,720
for the enormous riches discovered in his tomb,

383
00:22:56,721 --> 00:23:02,600
but also for the mysteries surrounding his life and death.

384
00:23:02,601 --> 00:23:05,500
Today's technology has attempted to uncover some

385
00:23:05,501 --> 00:23:07,760
of the answers to this legendary intrigue.

386
00:23:11,080 --> 00:23:13,420
Fascination has surrounded Tutte

387
00:23:13,421 --> 00:23:15,960
since British archaeologist Howard Carter first

388
00:23:15,961 --> 00:23:20,200
discovered him in 1922.

389
00:23:20,201 --> 00:23:22,060
When he discovered the tomb and actually

390
00:23:22,061 --> 00:23:25,444
began to publicize these finds, this news galloped around the world.

391
00:23:25,445 --> 00:23:26,340
I mean, front page

392
00:23:26,341 --> 00:23:31,460
news in New York and in Sydney and in Cairo and in Paris were all talking about this sensational

393
00:23:31,461 --> 00:23:33,680
find. It really was Tutmania.

394
00:23:33,681 --> 00:23:38,580
In the beginning, Tutmania was motivated by the stores of riches

395
00:23:38,581 --> 00:23:40,235
found in his tomb.

396
00:23:40,236 --> 00:23:47,040
There were over 5,000 objects put into this very small tomb with King Tut.

397
00:23:47,041 --> 00:23:53,000
There's gold, there's alabaster, there's ivory, jewelry and precious stones that still dazzle your eye.

398
00:23:53,360 --> 00:23:57,800
And one of the most remarkable things is how well preserved they are, as though they were made yesterday.

399
00:23:59,700 --> 00:24:05,880
One of the most celebrated relics unearthed was the gold burial mask that was placed on Tut's face.

400
00:24:07,260 --> 00:24:15,060
This particular death mask is one of the most spectacular objects ever found in anywhere, anywhere and from any culture.

401
00:24:15,061 --> 00:24:19,740
made of gold and a variety of other metals, including silver,

402
00:24:20,040 --> 00:24:23,640
but also with stones such as onyx, turquoise, obsidian,

403
00:24:23,800 --> 00:24:25,880
a variety of different elements put together

404
00:24:25,881 --> 00:24:29,320
to make an absolutely gorgeous death mask.

405
00:24:29,700 --> 00:24:32,940
In a short time, supernatural mystery

406
00:24:32,941 --> 00:24:34,720
would accompany this sublime treasure.

407
00:24:36,380 --> 00:24:39,920
Legend has it that those who entered the tomb became ill

408
00:24:39,921 --> 00:24:42,020
and died shortly thereafter.

409
00:24:42,021 --> 00:24:45,060
The first individual to die that started the legend

410
00:24:45,160 --> 00:24:47,400
of the curse of the mummy was Lord Carnarvon.

411
00:24:47,500 --> 00:24:50,500
He was the man who financed Carter's digs.

412
00:24:50,600 --> 00:24:54,540
He died about two months after Carter opened the chamber

413
00:24:54,640 --> 00:24:55,840
that had the mummy in it.

414
00:24:55,940 --> 00:24:57,800
And so the legend was born.

415
00:24:57,900 --> 00:25:00,500
And then over time, anybody who was associated with the project

416
00:25:00,600 --> 00:25:03,300
who died suddenly was ascribed to be a victim

417
00:25:03,400 --> 00:25:05,480
of the mummy's curse.

418
00:25:05,580 --> 00:25:08,580
For decades, the myth endured.

419
00:25:08,680 --> 00:25:12,020
Hollywood even cashed in on the curse.

420
00:25:12,021 --> 00:25:14,020
I think the mummy, as a horror figure,

421
00:25:14,120 --> 00:25:16,600
really becomes prominent after the discovery

422
00:25:16,700 --> 00:25:18,060
of King Tut's tomb.

423
00:25:18,160 --> 00:25:20,760
There was all this talk about the curse of King Tut.

424
00:25:20,860 --> 00:25:23,960
And then you get the idea of mummies as things that kill

425
00:25:24,060 --> 00:25:25,060
people.

426
00:25:25,160 --> 00:25:28,400
So I think it's really after Tutankhamen's tomb was discovered.

427
00:25:28,500 --> 00:25:32,080
The curse may have struck gold at the box office,

428
00:25:32,180 --> 00:25:35,140
but science has some far different theories about what

429
00:25:35,240 --> 00:25:37,680
might cause death to those who enter tombs.

430
00:25:37,681 --> 00:25:38,752
enter tombs.

431
00:25:38,753 --> 00:25:45,720
There apparently are some very real scientific reasons that are surfacing now.

432
00:25:46,340 --> 00:25:50,420
Most archaeologists these days who open sealed tombs in ancient Egypt actually let the tombs

433
00:25:50,421 --> 00:25:54,820
breathe, so to speak, for a few days because there may well be buildups of toxic gases,

434
00:25:55,040 --> 00:25:56,133
other toxic materials.

435
00:25:56,134 --> 00:25:59,460
These are chambers that have been sealed for countless generations.

436
00:26:00,220 --> 00:26:06,360
They have decomposing bodies in them, foodstuffs, the resins and the other chemicals that were

437
00:26:06,361 --> 00:26:08,820
and used during the lummification process,

438
00:26:08,920 --> 00:26:11,700
all of that can contribute to a pretty risky environment.

439
00:26:11,800 --> 00:26:13,700
So archaeologists actually let the tombs breathe

440
00:26:13,800 --> 00:26:15,900
before they go in.

441
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,400
So perhaps science has solved the mystery

442
00:26:18,500 --> 00:26:21,900
of the curse of King Tut.

443
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:25,420
Likewise, science has been put to use to solve another enduring

444
00:26:25,520 --> 00:26:31,620
King Tut mystery, a royal murder.

445
00:26:31,720 --> 00:26:35,260
The mystery of King Tut's death began at the time

446
00:26:35,261 --> 00:26:38,860
at the time that he was found, because they discovered

447
00:26:38,960 --> 00:26:40,000
that he was a youth.

448
00:26:40,100 --> 00:26:42,400
And we needed to find out exactly how he died.

449
00:26:42,500 --> 00:26:43,900
Did he die a natural death?

450
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:44,900
Was he murdered?

451
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:46,400
Was he killed?

452
00:26:46,500 --> 00:26:51,280
The boy king ruled ancient Egypt about 3,500 years ago,

453
00:26:51,380 --> 00:26:53,280
after inheriting the throne from his father

454
00:26:53,380 --> 00:26:57,040
at the tender age of nine.

455
00:26:57,140 --> 00:27:00,720
10 years later, his reign was cut short.

456
00:27:00,820 --> 00:27:03,080
And there were a number of contenders for the throne.

457
00:27:03,081 --> 00:27:05,740
So it's certainly within the realm of possibility.

458
00:27:06,020 --> 00:27:09,940
We know that there were other assassinations in Egyptian history of pharaohs.

459
00:27:09,980 --> 00:27:13,880
So it would certainly be possible that Tutankhamen could have been murdered.

460
00:27:14,500 --> 00:27:19,860
In an effort to solve the mystery, Tut's mummy was X-rayed in 1968,

461
00:27:20,220 --> 00:27:24,720
which revealed an intriguing hole in the back of his head.

462
00:27:26,500 --> 00:27:29,820
The idea that Tut might have died in a palace coup

463
00:27:29,821 --> 00:27:33,880
really took hold after the X-ray of the mummy

464
00:27:33,881 --> 00:27:36,760
and revealed a chip of bone driven up into the back of his head.

465
00:27:36,840 --> 00:27:37,900
He might have been bludgeoned.

466
00:27:40,520 --> 00:27:45,020
In 2005, Tuts remains underwent a CT scan,

467
00:27:45,360 --> 00:27:48,580
which produced detailed cross-section images.

468
00:27:49,400 --> 00:27:53,180
The discovery in the CAT scan about the hole in the back of the head

469
00:27:53,181 --> 00:27:56,740
indicated that it was part of the mummification process.

470
00:27:56,741 --> 00:28:00,640
In fact, in the 18th dynasty, many of the mummies had holes

471
00:28:00,740 --> 00:28:02,420
in the back of the head they've discovered

472
00:28:02,520 --> 00:28:04,520
when they do these CAT scans.

473
00:28:04,620 --> 00:28:09,180
So what caused the Boy King to die so young?

474
00:28:09,280 --> 00:28:12,560
The CT scan may have also revealed the real cause

475
00:28:12,660 --> 00:28:16,000
of Tut's demise.

476
00:28:16,100 --> 00:28:19,460
The latest discoveries with CT scanning

477
00:28:19,560 --> 00:28:23,240
seem to have suggested that there was perhaps a fracture

478
00:28:23,241 --> 00:28:28,800
fracture in his leg and that perhaps an infection that set in later could have killed him.

479
00:28:28,801 --> 00:28:35,840
We know that even a toothache, the cavity, could eventually lead to someone's death.

480
00:28:35,920 --> 00:28:41,040
And in fact, some of the pharaohs seem to have died from infected teeth.

481
00:28:41,660 --> 00:28:46,280
This new evidence has essentially ended the investigation into Tut's murder.

482
00:28:47,560 --> 00:28:49,680
The case for murder is closed.

483
00:28:49,681 --> 00:28:54,180
100% of the population of Egyptologists believe he was not murdered.

484
00:28:54,460 --> 00:28:57,660
In addition to finding the cause of King Tut's death,

485
00:28:58,940 --> 00:29:02,380
technology has also given us clues as to what he looked like.

486
00:29:04,580 --> 00:29:06,540
Using facial reconstruction,

487
00:29:07,660 --> 00:29:11,180
much like law enforcement uses today to help solve murder cases,

488
00:29:12,340 --> 00:29:15,580
scientists have created a lifelike model of the Egyptian king.

489
00:29:15,581 --> 00:29:23,580
The CT scan images were used to construct a plastic model of Tut's skull.

490
00:29:23,581 --> 00:29:30,580
Then, using clay, a sculptor molded layers of musculature and skin.

491
00:29:30,581 --> 00:29:33,580
An artist fleshed out details.

492
00:29:33,581 --> 00:29:38,580
Eyebrow thickness, nose, lips, and the approximate shape of Tut's ears.

493
00:29:38,581 --> 00:29:43,580
Finally, glass eyes, hair, and even historically accurate makeup

494
00:29:43,581 --> 00:29:45,700
completed the reconstruction.

495
00:29:48,040 --> 00:29:51,840
This is believed to be what King Tut actually looked like.

496
00:29:57,160 --> 00:30:00,720
I think because of the technology that underlies this reconstruction,

497
00:30:01,080 --> 00:30:06,180
it's as close as anybody is going to get to what Tut looked like in life.

498
00:30:08,120 --> 00:30:12,160
However, the study of the boy king's remains may be far from over.

499
00:30:12,161 --> 00:30:15,160
Can technology reveal even more?

500
00:30:15,161 --> 00:30:20,160
The fascination of Tut endures, and I think as new technologies evolve,

501
00:30:20,161 --> 00:30:24,160
he will be a natural subject to apply those new technologies

502
00:30:24,161 --> 00:30:27,160
and unlock pieces of the story we can't even guess at yet.

503
00:30:27,161 --> 00:30:32,160
King Tut has always received the finest care and scientific study.

504
00:30:34,160 --> 00:30:39,160
But for centuries, countless mummies were desecrated, stolen,

505
00:30:39,161 --> 00:30:41,160
or simply lost and forgotten.

506
00:30:42,160 --> 00:30:51,140
Had the ancient Egyptians known the degree of shocking desecration

507
00:30:51,141 --> 00:30:52,960
to which their mummies would be subject,

508
00:30:54,300 --> 00:30:57,680
they might have thought twice about embalming them for eternity.

509
00:30:59,900 --> 00:31:03,160
Through the ages, mummies have been used for all kinds of things.

510
00:31:04,180 --> 00:31:08,300
People believed that ground-up mummy itself could give you immortality,

511
00:31:08,640 --> 00:31:11,220
so apothecaries in Europe, pharmacists,

512
00:31:11,221 --> 00:31:14,980
started grinding up mummies and using it as a medical ingredient

513
00:31:14,981 --> 00:31:16,360
to help the sick.

514
00:31:17,300 --> 00:31:19,700
During the Civil War, there was a shortage of paper.

515
00:31:20,340 --> 00:31:23,060
So the linen from mummies, the mummy bandages,

516
00:31:23,320 --> 00:31:27,600
were shipped over to America and pulped and made into paper

517
00:31:27,601 --> 00:31:29,120
for various industries.

518
00:31:30,380 --> 00:31:33,360
19th century aristocrats entertained themselves

519
00:31:33,361 --> 00:31:36,280
by buying mummies and holding what were called

520
00:31:36,281 --> 00:31:39,280
observation sessions for public audiences.

521
00:31:41,220 --> 00:31:43,720
People would unwrap them, and they'd want to see

522
00:31:43,820 --> 00:31:44,960
what's inside the bandages.

523
00:31:45,060 --> 00:31:47,160
They certainly want to see what the face looked like.

524
00:31:47,260 --> 00:31:49,660
And really, we've lost a lot of knowledge and information

525
00:31:49,760 --> 00:31:50,860
about that.

526
00:31:50,960 --> 00:31:52,060
I mean, I think if any poor ancient Egyptian

527
00:31:52,160 --> 00:31:53,660
knew what was going to happen to his mummy that way,

528
00:31:53,760 --> 00:31:55,760
I think he'd be very unhappy about it.

529
00:31:55,860 --> 00:31:59,080
This poor fellow may have been the guest of honor,

530
00:31:59,180 --> 00:32:03,980
purchased for an unwrapping party held in a private home.

531
00:32:04,080 --> 00:32:06,940
It's very probable that this mummy was unwrapped

532
00:32:07,040 --> 00:32:09,240
during Victorian times as part of one

533
00:32:09,241 --> 00:32:13,040
of one of those parlor games where they would just unwrap

534
00:32:13,140 --> 00:32:16,240
the linens to see what amulets or jewels or treasures

535
00:32:16,340 --> 00:32:18,480
would fall off of the mummy's body.

536
00:32:18,580 --> 00:32:20,580
They were fascinated with what they might find.

537
00:32:20,680 --> 00:32:21,580
It was a treasure hunt.

538
00:32:21,680 --> 00:32:24,020
It was a lot easier than going to Egypt

539
00:32:24,120 --> 00:32:25,320
and digging through the sand.

540
00:32:25,420 --> 00:32:27,320
They could simply unwrap these linens

541
00:32:27,420 --> 00:32:31,000
and see what would fall out, and then it belonged to them.

542
00:32:31,100 --> 00:32:34,500
Mummies were bought and sold like any other commodity.

543
00:32:34,600 --> 00:32:36,140
Many were lost over time.

544
00:32:36,141 --> 00:32:42,140
In 1860, a collection of mummies was purchased by a museum in

545
00:32:42,141 --> 00:32:44,140
Niagara Falls.

546
00:32:44,141 --> 00:32:49,140
No one knew that in that collection rested one of the

547
00:32:49,141 --> 00:32:53,140
most powerful men ever to walk the banks of the Nile.

548
00:32:53,141 --> 00:32:58,140
The Niagara Falls Museum was really less of a true museum

549
00:32:58,141 --> 00:33:02,140
than a kind of side show, where it had two-headed animals and

550
00:33:02,141 --> 00:33:05,140
the barrels people went over the falls in.

551
00:33:05,141 --> 00:33:08,240
and kind of bizarre things like that.

552
00:33:08,880 --> 00:33:12,880
But one mummy in this collection of oddities seemed to stand out.

553
00:33:14,460 --> 00:33:16,540
As a seven-year-old or eight-year-old boy,

554
00:33:16,700 --> 00:33:18,220
I went to the museum and saw this mummy

555
00:33:18,221 --> 00:33:21,420
and stood face-to-face, if you will, with this ancient person.

556
00:33:21,860 --> 00:33:23,620
The first thing that made people look at this

557
00:33:23,621 --> 00:33:25,040
was the pose of the mummy

558
00:33:25,041 --> 00:33:28,080
and the obvious high quality of the process.

559
00:33:28,860 --> 00:33:32,660
The real tip-off was that the position of the arms

560
00:33:32,661 --> 00:33:34,460
was crossed over the chest.

561
00:33:34,461 --> 00:33:37,460
This was a posture reserved only for pharaohs.

562
00:33:38,460 --> 00:33:40,060
For more than 100 years,

563
00:33:40,400 --> 00:33:42,000
this mummy entertained the masses,

564
00:33:42,860 --> 00:33:46,640
until the Niagara Falls Museum closed in 1998.

565
00:33:47,940 --> 00:33:51,400
Its mummies were sold to the Michael C. Carlos Museum

566
00:33:51,401 --> 00:33:53,300
at Emory University in Atlanta.

567
00:33:55,740 --> 00:33:58,420
There, the mysterious cross-armed body

568
00:33:58,421 --> 00:34:01,140
finally received the high-tech attention needed

569
00:34:01,141 --> 00:34:03,260
for a thorough background check.

570
00:34:04,460 --> 00:34:08,460
So once it got here, we began consulting with experts

571
00:34:08,461 --> 00:34:10,460
from all over the world.

572
00:34:10,461 --> 00:34:12,460
However, the pharaoh connection couldn't be established

573
00:34:12,461 --> 00:34:16,460
with DNA, since Egyptian authorities were reluctant to give

574
00:34:16,461 --> 00:34:19,460
samples from their royal mummies.

575
00:34:19,461 --> 00:34:24,460
Instead, non-invasive CT scans and x-rays were compared

576
00:34:24,461 --> 00:34:27,460
to other known ancient pharaohs.

577
00:34:27,461 --> 00:34:30,460
Just who was this mummy?

578
00:34:30,461 --> 00:34:33,460
Stunningly, one candidate stood out.

579
00:34:33,461 --> 00:34:35,960
Ramses I.

580
00:34:36,060 --> 00:34:39,460
The style of the wrappings matched other important royal

581
00:34:39,560 --> 00:34:46,800
mummies, most closely that of Seti I and Ramses II,

582
00:34:46,900 --> 00:34:52,420
son and grandson of the missing mummy of Ramses I.

583
00:34:52,520 --> 00:34:56,920
The CT scan was very useful because it revealed inside

584
00:34:57,020 --> 00:35:01,060
the mummy two very tightly coiled pieces of linen

585
00:35:01,061 --> 00:35:05,060
in the body cavity used as filling to help sort of plump

586
00:35:05,061 --> 00:35:08,060
out the body once the internal organs were removed.

587
00:35:08,061 --> 00:35:12,060
This is paralleled only by the mummy of Seti I,

588
00:35:12,061 --> 00:35:14,060
the son of Ramses I.

589
00:35:14,061 --> 00:35:18,060
So that seemed to clinch father and son together.

590
00:35:18,061 --> 00:35:22,060
We came to the conclusion that it by far was most likely the

591
00:35:22,061 --> 00:35:24,060
mummy of Ramses I.

592
00:35:24,061 --> 00:35:27,060
The discovery of Ramses I filled a long-standing gap

593
00:35:27,061 --> 00:35:30,060
in the historical lineage of Egyptian pharaohs.

594
00:35:30,061 --> 00:35:37,060
In order for the great king to take his place with other royal mummies,

595
00:35:37,061 --> 00:35:43,060
the Carlos Museum voluntarily sent him back to Egypt,

596
00:35:43,061 --> 00:35:48,060
where he received a regal reception.

597
00:35:59,200 --> 00:36:02,080
Without this technology, we never would have been able to make

598
00:36:02,180 --> 00:36:03,980
that determination.

599
00:36:04,080 --> 00:36:07,840
Being the mummy of Ramses I, giving it back its identity.

600
00:36:11,980 --> 00:36:14,080
Just as scientists use technology

601
00:36:14,081 --> 00:36:16,180
to give Ramses his name and his honor.

602
00:36:17,720 --> 00:36:23,340
Other scientists use technology to preserve and protect legions of unnamed mummies,

603
00:36:23,780 --> 00:36:27,200
who are at risk of literally crumbling to dust.

604
00:36:38,040 --> 00:36:43,460
Locked away in sealed tombs, mummies have survived for thousands of years.

605
00:36:44,080 --> 00:36:51,460
But now that they're out in the modern world, how will they fare?

606
00:36:54,460 --> 00:36:58,160
Restoring and preserving mummies is kind of in its infancy.

607
00:36:58,540 --> 00:37:06,300
The biggest challenge that we face with the mummies is that the linens and the bodies themselves have become fragile and brittle and often broken over time.

608
00:37:07,580 --> 00:37:13,040
Preservationists use a sheer nylon material to help keep mummies from literally falling to pieces.

609
00:37:14,080 --> 00:37:18,340
This newly developed technique holds the bandages and body parts together.

610
00:37:19,880 --> 00:37:23,380
This mummy has been prepared for display in the galleries

611
00:37:23,381 --> 00:37:26,920
by first carefully arranging the wrappings that are on the mummy

612
00:37:26,921 --> 00:37:30,060
and then covering the whole mummy with a sheer fabric

613
00:37:30,061 --> 00:37:34,680
that matches the color of the old linen and allows you to see through it,

614
00:37:35,080 --> 00:37:38,040
enabling you to see the wrappings as they were originally arranged,

615
00:37:38,340 --> 00:37:39,740
somewhat as a hairnet does.

616
00:37:39,741 --> 00:37:43,240
It allows those loose and now very brittle linens

617
00:37:43,241 --> 00:37:47,140
to stay in place around the mummy as it lies on display.

618
00:37:48,080 --> 00:37:50,320
The natrin, or salt compound,

619
00:37:50,460 --> 00:37:52,880
that was used by the Egyptians to preserve mummies

620
00:37:52,881 --> 00:37:56,320
today may cause a mummy to actually disintegrate.

621
00:37:58,200 --> 00:38:01,380
And if there are any changes in temperature and humidity,

622
00:38:01,760 --> 00:38:05,500
the salt that is naturally dissolved in the material

623
00:38:05,501 --> 00:38:09,420
will redissolve and migrate in or out,

624
00:38:09,421 --> 00:38:13,220
depending on if the air is drier or wetter than the object.

625
00:38:13,880 --> 00:38:18,000
And the movement of this salt back and forth actually erodes the surface.

626
00:38:18,160 --> 00:38:21,380
It actually causes things to explode from inside out.

627
00:38:21,940 --> 00:38:28,000
To control this problem, a specially designed computerized system at the Carlos Museum

628
00:38:28,001 --> 00:38:30,300
regulates temperature and humidity.

629
00:38:32,640 --> 00:38:36,720
The museum is kept at very specific environmental conditions,

630
00:38:36,721 --> 00:38:40,480
And in order to accomplish that, we use environmental controls

631
00:38:40,481 --> 00:38:43,720
through an air conditioning system that are set to maintain

632
00:38:43,721 --> 00:38:47,800
temperature at about 70 degrees, plus or minus 2 degrees Fahrenheit.

633
00:38:48,160 --> 00:38:51,140
And then we set the relative humidity around 50%,

634
00:38:51,141 --> 00:38:53,420
plus or minus 5% RH.

635
00:38:55,200 --> 00:38:58,840
Tiny organic microbes can also wreak havoc on a mummy.

636
00:39:01,260 --> 00:39:03,600
At the Egyptian Museum in Cairo,

637
00:39:03,601 --> 00:39:09,420
A common intruder was found resting alongside kings and queens.

638
00:39:10,840 --> 00:39:15,300
It was discovered that the mummies in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo were growing mold.

639
00:39:15,540 --> 00:39:16,720
They were literally getting moldy.

640
00:39:17,700 --> 00:39:21,900
Everyday household mold and fungus can destroy the most revered pharaoh.

641
00:39:24,600 --> 00:39:30,060
If mummy is not protected, mummy could be disintegrated in a matter of a couple of years.

642
00:39:30,061 --> 00:39:37,060
Shin Maikawa is a senior scientist at the Getty Conservation Institute in Los Angeles, California.

643
00:39:37,061 --> 00:39:44,060
He was asked by the Cairo Museum to help solve the problem of the moldy mummies.

644
00:39:44,061 --> 00:39:52,060
Three things affecting mummies are temperature, relative humidity and oxygen.

645
00:39:52,061 --> 00:39:57,060
Oxygen is one of the largest contributors to the breakdown of organic material.

646
00:39:57,061 --> 00:40:06,060
because it provides the fuel that allows microbes like mold and fungi to breed and multiply.

647
00:40:06,061 --> 00:40:12,060
To attack the problem, Maikawa designed an oxygen-free display case.

648
00:40:12,061 --> 00:40:17,060
This is a small prototype of the custom-built case.

649
00:40:17,061 --> 00:40:25,060
The goal of display cases is to provide stable, dry environment for mummy without oxygen

650
00:40:25,061 --> 00:40:32,200
oxygen so that we can eliminate both stress from humidity variation and possibility of

651
00:40:32,201 --> 00:40:34,060
fungi attack.

652
00:40:34,061 --> 00:40:39,260
To create an environment good for mummies and bad for microbes, three additional materials

653
00:40:39,261 --> 00:40:41,260
are needed.

654
00:40:41,261 --> 00:40:51,540
A compound of essentially powdered iron absorbs oxygen in the air, while activated charcoal

655
00:40:51,541 --> 00:40:55,966
granules, filter out impurities and pollutants.

656
00:40:55,967 --> 00:41:00,580
And silicon gel caplets help control the humidity

657
00:41:00,581 --> 00:41:06,603
or moisture. They're all placed inside the display case.

658
00:41:06,604 --> 00:41:10,260
Then the case is sealed, airtight.

659
00:41:11,620 --> 00:41:15,520
Now the environment of the case is made inhospitable to microbes.

660
00:41:15,521 --> 00:41:21,420
as oxygen is removed

661
00:41:21,421 --> 00:41:25,420
and pure nitrogen is pumped in.

662
00:41:26,700 --> 00:41:30,920
Nothing live can possibly survive in nitrogen.

663
00:41:31,300 --> 00:41:32,680
So once you put a mummy in there,

664
00:41:33,040 --> 00:41:34,240
in an atmosphere of nitrogen,

665
00:41:34,560 --> 00:41:36,920
no fungus, no bacteria, no molds,

666
00:41:36,921 --> 00:41:38,920
it'll be preserved just perfectly.

667
00:41:39,800 --> 00:41:43,260
The Egyptian museum has now placed their royal mummies

668
00:41:43,261 --> 00:41:45,400
in Maikawa's specially designed cases.

669
00:41:45,520 --> 00:41:50,460
And the common mold problem has been vanquished.

670
00:41:51,560 --> 00:41:55,660
This play case is very easy to build, very inexpensive,

671
00:41:56,240 --> 00:41:58,320
and we can keep the mummy forever.

672
00:42:01,520 --> 00:42:07,340
And after all, forever was the goal of the ancient Egyptians in the first place.

673
00:42:10,960 --> 00:42:13,960
They believed that speaking the names of the dead

674
00:42:13,961 --> 00:42:18,880
and visiting their remains would give the deceased an eternal afterlife.

675
00:42:22,260 --> 00:42:26,300
Today, with the public intensely interested in mummies,

676
00:42:26,980 --> 00:42:29,740
it appears they may have succeeded.

677
00:42:40,960 --> 00:42:43,080
There are always going to be surprises.

678
00:42:43,960 --> 00:42:47,760
You don't have to speak the language to be able to interact with people.

679
00:42:49,260 --> 00:42:51,680
Knowing that can make you look at history in a new way.

